 38 (119th):** she is an original cosponsor of the concealed-carry reciprocity bill. That meets the \"loosen rules on carrying\" part.\n- **H.J.Res. 44 (118th):** she voted Yea (219–210, in-term, June 2023) to nullify the ATF's new stabilizing-brace rule. I read that as the vote against a new restriction.\n\nRung 5 isn't reached: no record repeals a major federal gun law.\n\n**Passages that don't count:**\n- **H.R. 2189 (Yea, 2026):** coded `adjacent`. It removes less-than-lethal devices like Tasers from firearm regulation, and those devices aren't firearms.\n- **Her campaign issues page:** coded third-party characterization and rhetorical. It's written in the third person (\"Erin is a firm believer…\") and names no policy.\n\n**The weak point:** the brace rule was already in force when the House voted, so nullifying it could be read as repealing an NFA rule, not blocking a new one. The repeal reading points toward rung 5's \"repeal major restrictions\" clause rather than rung 4. If reviewers take that reading, the second passage drops out and the row becomes BLANK `direction-only`.\n\n**Shape coding to check:** on their own, the H.R. 38 and H.J.Res. 44 passages show only a direction. I marked them `chair-shaped` because the annex seats rung 4 on the pair, and the schema won't accept a chair resting on `direction-only` passages.\n\n**`needs_source` lists:**\n- her own first-person words on gun restrictions;\n- the introduced text of H.R. 38. The page in the sources is the committee-amended version, not the one she cosponsored on 2025-01-03.","ttft_ms":46140,"type":"result","duration_ms":76124,"uuid":"57325c4c-d309-4004-bf9e-d80d137c2ee9","ttft_stream_ms":1941,"time_to_request_ms":79,"first_content_frame_ms":1941,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
